      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{2}^{y-1},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{2}^{y-1}=0\\cdot {2}^{1-1}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{2}^{y-1}=1\\cdot {2}^{1-1}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{2}^{y-1}=2\\cdot {2}^{2-1}=4 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+4=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $3$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈6.$",
