      "dom_id": "question-120895",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(5).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(5).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=5$ is $4,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y-{y}^{2}=2(1)-{1}^{2}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 2=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+2=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+2=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y-{y}^{2}=2(3)-{3}^{2}=-3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-3\\cdot 2=-6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+2=5 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3-6=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $-3$ | $-6$ |\n| $5$ | $-3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(5)≈-3.$",
